DXWEB AND DXWEB ADMIN - INSTALLATION, UPDATES AND RECOVERY
=========================================================

Review edition: 27 October 2026
Verified manager: dxweb-manager.pl v0.49
Tested applications: DXWeb 3.0.1 and DXWeb Admin 1.0.3

1. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
-------------------

This is the sole installation manual for DXWeb and DXWeb Admin.
Both applications are part of the DXSpider Git tree in /spider.
The normal procedure uses dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl. The manager
configures systemd services and, optionally, an example Nginx
configuration. It does NOT copy or update application files: these
come exclusively from the existing Git tree.

Direct installation is the default, validated mode.
It does not require Nginx. Nginx is documented as a technical example
without official reverse-proxy support; it has not been revalidated
in this version.

Run installation operations as root, without sudo.
The account running the manager need not be the service account.
sysop:spider is recommended where it has the required permissions.
The manager can retain the identity of installed units or inherit it
from the DXSpider service. To specify it explicitly, use
--user sysop --group spider.

2. FILES AND DIAGRAMS
---------------------

  /spider/INSTALL_DXWEB.es          Spanish installation manual
  /spider/INSTALL_DXWEB.en          British English installation manual
  /spider/dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl    Service manager
  /spider/dxweb/install.pl          Tree-copy installer
  /spider/dxweb/                    DXWeb application
  /spider/dxweb-admin/              DXWeb Admin application

Configuration diagram in English:
  /spider/dxweb/docs/DXWeb_Configuration_EN.png

Check the diagram against the ports and modes described here before
using it as a deployment reference.

The dxweb/install.pl installer serves a different purpose: it copies
a DXWeb source tree to a destination and retains the previous tree.
Do not use it for a normal update of code already integrated in
/spider.

3. MODES AND PORTS
------------------

  Mode                 DXWeb          Admin          DXSpider IntMsg
  -------------------  -------------- -------------- ---------------
  DIRECT HTTP          0.0.0.0:7380   0.0.0.0:7381   127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, HTTP backend  127.0.0.1:7480 127.0.0.1:7481 127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, public        port 7380      port 7381      not exposed

In direct mode, --scheme https configures TLS in the applications.
In Nginx mode, --scheme https configures TLS in the proxy, while
the applications continue to serve HTTP on loopback.

Port 27754 is not intended for browsers.
The default is 127.0.0.1:27754. An update preserves DXS_HOST and
DXS_PORT from existing units.

3.1. CONFIGURATION VARIABLES AND PORTS
--------------------------------------

The following variables are set as Environment= entries in systemd
units. The manager generates them according to the selected mode.
There is no need to edit them manually when using the installer.

  Variable              Application      Purpose
  --------------------  ---------------  -----------------------------
  DXS_HOST              Both             IntMsg listener host
  DXS_PORT              Both             IntMsg listener port
  HTTP_HOST             DXWeb            HTTP listening address
  HTTP_PORT             DXWeb            HTTP listening port
  DXWEB_HOST            DXWeb Admin      Admin listening address
  DXWEB_PORT            DXWeb Admin      Admin listening port
  DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT     DXWeb Admin      DXWeb backend port
  DXWEB_CLIENT_HOST     DXWeb Admin      DXWeb connection host
  DXWEB_CLIENT_SCHEME   DXWeb Admin      DXWeb connection scheme
  DXWEB_SCHEME          Both             HTTP or HTTPS scheme
  MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY    Both             HTTP proxy trust setting
  DXSPIDER_ROOT         Both             DXSpider tree path
  DXWEB_TLS_CERT        Both             Direct TLS certificate
  DXWEB_TLS_KEY         Both             Direct TLS private key

Typical values for a new installation:

  Variable              Direct HTTP      With Nginx
  --------------------  ---------------  -----------------------------
  DXS_HOST              127.0.0.1        127.0.0.1
  DXS_PORT              27754            27754
  HTTP_HOST             0.0.0.0          127.0.0.1
  HTTP_PORT             7380             7480
  DXWEB_HOST            0.0.0.0          127.0.0.1
  DXWEB_PORT            7381             7481
  DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT     7380             7480
  MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY    0                1

DXS_HOST and DXS_PORT identify the DXSpider IntMsg service, not the
browser's HTTP access point. During an update, the manager preserves
their values if they are already present in installed units.

HTTP_HOST and HTTP_PORT apply exclusively to DXWeb.
DXWEB_HOST and DXWEB_PORT apply to DXWeb Admin. DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT
tells Admin which port the DXWeb backend uses; it must not be
confused with the proxy's public port.

DXWEB_CLIENT_HOST and DXWEB_CLIENT_SCHEME are generated according
to the selected mode and scheme. In Nginx mode, the applications
listen over HTTP on loopback even when public access uses HTTPS.

MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=0 is for direct access; 1 is for access through
a trusted HTTP proxy. For manual configurations, see section 7
on headers and the real client IP address. Do not enable this
trust for direct clients.

DXWEB_TLS_CERT and DXWEB_TLS_KEY are included only when the
applications serve HTTPS directly. With Nginx, the certificate
and key are configured in the proxy, not the HTTP backends.

To inspect the values actually installed:

  systemctl cat dxweb.service
  systemctl cat dxweb-admin.service

The examples above describe new installations. Existing units may
retain different local values.

4. PRE-INSTALLATION CHECKS
--------------------------

Run as root on the DXSpider server.

  cd /spider || exit 1
  ls -ld dxweb dxweb-admin
  perl -c dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --help
  id sysop
  getent group spider

If the services use another account, check that account and its
group instead of sysop:spider.

Do not proceed if components or required permissions are missing,
or if the syntax check fails. Do not discard local changes to force
an update. Check the actual identity of the services:

  systemctl cat dxspider.service
  systemctl cat dxweb.service
  systemctl cat dxweb-admin.service

It is normal for the last two units not to exist on first install.
Do not change the identity of existing services without first
checking access to /spider and the required operational data.

5. FIRST DIRECT HTTP INSTALLATION
---------------------------------

First review the plan, which does not modify services:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode direct \
    --scheme http --user sysop --group spider

If the plan is as expected, install:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode direct \
    --scheme http --user sysop --group spider --apply

The manager creates or updates the systemd units. There is no need
to copy .service files from the repository manually.

6. DIRECT UPDATES
-----------------

DXWeb and DXWeb Admin are part of the DXSpider repository.
Their files are updated alongside the rest of the project using
the normal DXSpider update procedure.

There is no need to download or update these components separately.

After updating DXSpider, if DXWeb or DXWeb Admin has changed, run
the installation manager using the procedure in section 5.

Review the plan first and apply the changes if it is correct.

The manager updates service configuration when necessary and
restarts the services to use the updated code.

DXSpider does not need to be restarted for an update affecting
only DXWeb or DXWeb Admin.

7. INSTALLATION AND UPDATES WITH NGINX
--------------------------------------

NOT AN OFFICIALLY SUPPORTED EXAMPLE. Manager v0.49 can generate an
Nginx configuration on the same host as DXWeb and DXWeb Admin.
A proxy on another server or use under URL subpaths has not been
validated here.

Check that Nginx is installed, review its configuration and back it up:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  nginx -t
  ls -la /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
  mkdir -p /root/dxweb-nginx-backup
  cp -a /etc/nginx/nginx.conf /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-available /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-enabled /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/

Do not delete or overwrite unrelated sites. If the site
/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb.conf already exists, the manager
rejects the Nginx installation and requires manual migration.
Do not bypass this by deleting configuration.

For HTTP, replace web.example.net with the actual DNS name:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme http --hostname web.example.net \
    --user sysop --group spider

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme http --hostname web.example.net \
    --user sysop --group spider --apply

HTTPS requires a real certificate and private key which match and
cover the hostname. The manager checks these details:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme https --hostname web.example.net \
    --cert /etc/ssl/dxweb/fullchain.pem \
    --key /etc/ssl/dxweb/privkey.pem \
    --user sysop --group spider

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme https --hostname web.example.net \
    --cert /etc/ssl/dxweb/fullchain.pem \
    --key /etc/ssl/dxweb/privkey.pem \
    --user sysop --group spider --apply

The certificate paths are examples; do not use them literally.
The manager does not issue certificates. The generated Nginx
configuration is stored at
/etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb-installer.conf, with a link at
/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb-installer.conf.

The manager generates two server blocks on ports 7380 and 7381,
proxying to 127.0.0.1:7480 and 127.0.0.1:7481. It configures
WebSocket Upgrade and proxy headers. Keep internal ports
inaccessible from outside. Do not trust headers sent by clients.

MANUAL CONFIGURATION OF AN HTTP REVERSE PROXY

When DXWeb or DXWeb Admin is manually configured behind an HTTP
reverse proxy, set MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=1 in the relevant service.
The proxy must forward the real client IP address using X-Real-IP
or X-Forwarded-For.

Accept these headers only from trusted proxies to prevent clients
from spoofing their IP address. Check that DXWeb correctly identifies
the user's real IP address, since an incorrect address can affect
spot acceptance by other nodes.

The manager automatically sets MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=1 and the required
headers when --mode nginx is used. These manual instructions are for
other proxies or installations made without the manager.

To update an Nginx installation, retain the hostname, scheme,
certificate and key. Review the plan first, then apply it.
Do not mix a direct service with the Nginx proxy without migration.

8. INSTALLING ONLY ONE APPLICATION
----------------------------------

In direct mode, --components dxweb or --components admin can be used
instead of both. Keep the other plan and installation arguments.
In Nginx mode, the manager can preserve the other component's block
when a configuration managed by the installer exists and matches
the exact format it recognises. For a first Nginx installation,
--components both is recommended.

9. VERIFYING THE RESULT
-----------------------

  cd /spider || exit 1
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action status --components both
  systemctl is-active dxweb.service dxweb-admin.service
  systemctl show dxweb.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  systemctl show dxweb-admin.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  ss -lntp | grep -E ':(7380|7381|7480|7481)\b'

For a direct HTTP installation:

  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7380/software-version.json
  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7381/software-versions.json

In Nginx mode, check nginx -t and use the actual public URL with
its http or https scheme and ports 7380 and 7381.
Check spots, SYSOP authentication, WebSockets, supervision, filters
and administrative permissions in a browser. A successful HTTP
response does not replace these checks.

10. BACKUPS AND ROLLBACK
------------------------

The manager stores transactions involving changed configuration in:

  /var/backups/dxweb-installer/

Keep the exact transaction name. Before reverting, inspect its
manifest.json and the rollback plan. For example:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  ls -lt /var/backups/dxweb-installer/
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action rollback \
    --backup /var/backups/dxweb-installer/TRANSACTION

Only after reviewing the result, run:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action rollback \
    --backup /var/backups/dxweb-installer/TRANSACTION --apply

Rollback restores configuration and service state; it does NOT
change the Git code revision. If the transaction relates to a first
installation, the correct outcome may be removal of the systemd
units: LoadState=not-found and ActiveState=inactive.
The manager uses bounded shutdown and a safety checkpoint during
recovery. Do not repeat rollback or restore units manually without
first inspecting the resulting state and logs.

Do not delete databases, filters, certificates or local
configuration as a supposed recovery procedure.

11. LOGS AND DIAGNOSTICS
------------------------

  journalctl -u dxweb.service -n 100 --no-pager
  journalctl -u dxweb-admin.service -n 100 --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb.service --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb-admin.service --no-pager

With Nginx, also check:

  nginx -t
  journalctl -u nginx.service -n 100 --no-pager

If the manager detects an incompatible Nginx site, do not delete
the existing configuration. Prepare a migration with a backup.
If it reports NO CONFIG CHANGES, it may still be necessary to run
install --apply after updating the Git code.

12. TECHNICAL SYSTEMD EXAMPLES
------------------------------

Four independent examples can be distributed:

  dxweb.service.example
  dxweb-admin.service.example
  dxweb.service.nginx.example
  dxweb-admin.service.nginx.example

They are references for manual testing, NOT installation steps.
The manager generates the installed units directly and preserves
some pre-existing local options. The examples should therefore
represent a new installation, using sysop:spider, /spider as the
actual path, and DXS_HOST=127.0.0.1, DXS_PORT=27754. If /spider
is a symbolic link, the manager uses the resolved physical path;
the examples require that path to be adjusted before manual use.

The examples will be checked against the unit generator before
being added to the repository. Do not replace an operational unit
with an example without a backup and a review of the differences.

13. LIMITATIONS AND SECURITY
----------------------------

HTTPS is recommended whenever DXWeb or DXWeb Admin is accessible
from the Internet or public networks.

This recommendation is particularly important for DXWeb Admin,
as it provides administrative functions and requires authentication.

Unencrypted HTTP should be restricted to trusted local networks
or controlled test environments.

Nginx support is provided as a configuration example, without
guaranteeing compatibility in every scenario. External proxies,
URL subpaths and certificates must be validated separately.

Do not recursively change ownership or permissions under /spider
to resolve access problems.

Installation does not require stopping or reinstalling DXSpider.
Do not use this manual to administer other projects on the node.
